<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mthethwa Paramountcy</h1><p class="st-meta">1780 CE – 1818 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern Nguni paramountcy of Dingiswayo, whose military reorganisation and patronage of the young Shaka paved the way for the Zulu Kingdom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Mthethwa were settled near the coast between the Mfolozi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mhlathuze-river">Mhlathuze</a> rivers. Under Jobe in the late 18th century they were already a significant chiefdom, and after a succession dispute his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> returned from exile around 1795 to take power and expand it into a paramountcy over many neighbours.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> organised young men into age-based regiments and brought neighbouring chiefdoms under his authority, often leaving their ruling families in place. He encouraged trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a> served in his army and, with Dingiswayo's backing, became ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, a Mthethwa dependent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1818 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> was captured and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ndwandwe-kingdom">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a>. The Mthethwa paramountcy collapsed, and its people were absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a>'s expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mthethwa-paramountcy.jpg" alt="Mthethwa Paramountcy" width="320" height="202"><figcaption>Image: SFC9394 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Africa_topo_continent.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Year